<p>[Equations in this post may not look right (or appear at all) in your RSS reader. Go to <a href="https://leancrew.com/all-this/2026/10/squaring-the-triangle-with-help/">the original article</a> to see them rendered properly.]</p>
  <hr />
  <p>Yesterday, Numberphile released <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EgnAN0ZUl08&amp;t=609s">a delightful video</a> with James Grime (redundant, I know) in which he walks us through a geometry problem first posed by Henry Dudeney in 1902. It’s known as the “<a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/27635/pg27635-images.html#s26">Haberdasher’s Puzzle</a>” and is reproduced in Dudeney’s 1907 book <em>The Canterbury Puzzles</em>. The problem was solved by C.W. McElroy after Dudeney published it in a periodical called the <em>Weekly Dispatch</em>.</p>
<iframe allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" allowfullscreen="" frameborder="0" height="315" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/EgnAN0ZUl08?si=aLz6IkQ67JsbCwUR" title="YouTube video player" width="560"></iframe>
<p>The problem is to cut up an equilateral triangle into pieces that can be translated and rotated (not flipped) to fit together into a square. The minimum number of pieces that allow you to do this is four, and here’s the arrangement shown in the video:</p>
<p><img alt="Equilateral triangle pieces in video" class="ss" src="https://leancrew.com/all-this/images2026/20261025-Equilateral%20triangle%20pieces%20in%20video.jpg" title="Equilateral triangle pieces in video" width="80%"/></p>
<p>What James doesn’t do in the video is explain how you position the cuts. Dudeney shows a <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/27635/pg27635-images.html#s26">straightedge-and-compass construction</a> in his book, but I wanted an algebraic solution. This turned out to be more complicated than I anticipated, but I had Mathematica to keep track of the messy algebra.</p>
<p>Let’s start with the equilateral triangle and its component pieces. The points that define the vertices of the triangle and cut lines are given the labels <em>A</em> through <em>J</em> (to avoid confusion, <em>I</em> is omitted).</p>
<p><img alt="Equilateral triangle with cuts" class="ss" src="https://leancrew.com/all-this/images2026/20261026-Equilateral%20triangle%20with%20cuts.png" title="Equilateral triangle with cuts" width="80%"/></p>
<p>The lines <em>GJ</em> and <em>DH</em> are perpendicular to Line <em>EF</em>.</p>
<p>To make the explanation simpler, let’s jump to the end and show how to make a square out of these components. The fun way to do it is in a series of rotations:</p>
<ol>
<li>Rotate the green piece 180° about Point <em>G</em>.</li>
<li>Rotate the green and orange pieces together 180° about Point <em>E</em>.</li>
<li>Rotate the green, orange, and blue pieces together 180° about Point <em>D</em>.</li>
</ol>
<p>Here’s an illustration of the steps:</p>
<p><img alt="Rotations from triangle to square" class="ss" src="https://leancrew.com/all-this/images2026/20261026-Rotations%20from%20triangle%20to%20square.png" title="Rotations from triangle to square" width="80%"/></p>
<p>In this illustration, the red piece stays put through all the steps.</p>
<p>We can rotate the square to make its sides horizontal and vertical and label the various points to show where they ended up.</p>
<p><img alt="Aligned square" class="ss" src="https://leancrew.com/all-this/images2026/20261026-Aligned%20square.png" title="Aligned square" width="80%"/></p>
<p>The vertices of the equilateral triangle have coalesced into a single point in the interior of the square. The right angles at <em>H</em> and <em>J</em> have split up to form the corners of the square.</p>
<p>Before we get into the algebra of the situation, let’s think about the various dimensions. The side lengths of the square, <em>s</em>, must be equal, so</p>
<p><!-- $$ s = 2 \bar{GJ} = 2 \bar{DH} = \bar{EJ} + \bar{EH} = \bar{FH} + \bar{FJ} $$ -->
<math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=".1em"></mspace><mover><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo accent="false" stretchy="true">——</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=".1em"></mspace><mover><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo accent="true" stretchy="true">——</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo accent="true" stretchy="true">——</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo accent="true" stretchy="true">——</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo accent="true" stretchy="true">——</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo accent="true" stretchy="true">——</mo></mover></mrow></math></p>
<p>A bit of algebra tells us that</p>
<p><!-- $$ \begin{align*}
2\,s &= \bar{EJ} + \bar{EH} + \bar{FH} + \bar{FJ}\\
 &= \left( \bar{EJ} + \bar{FJ} \right) + \left( \bar{FH} + \bar{EH} \right)
 \end{align*} $$ -->
<math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="right" style="text-align: right; padding-right: 0"><mn>2</mn><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mtd><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 0"><mo>=</mo><mover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo accent="true" stretchy="true">——</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo accent="true" stretchy="true">——</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo accent="true" stretchy="true">——</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo accent="true" stretchy="true">——</mo></mover></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="right" style="text-align: right; padding-right: 0"></mtd><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 0"><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo accent="true">——</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo accent="true" stretchy="true">——</mo></mover><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo accent="true" stretchy="true">——</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo accent="true" stretchy="true">——</mo></mover><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p>
<p>Looking back at the drawing of the equilateral triangle, we see that the terms in the parentheses make up Line <em>EF</em>. Therefore, the length of <em>EF</em> is the side length of the square, <em>s</em>.</p>
<p>Now it’s time for some analytic geometry. We’ll put the origin of our <em>xy</em> coordinate system at Point <em>A</em>, and we’ll assume each side of the equilateral triangle has unit length. The coordinates are:</p>
<p><!-- $$ \begin{align*}
A:\quad &(0, 0) \\
B:\quad &(1, 0)\\
C:\quad &\left(\frac{1}{2}, \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \right)
\end{align*} $$ -->
<math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="right" style="text-align: right; padding-right: 0"><mi>A</mi><mo>:</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace></mtd><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 0"><mo form="prefix" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="false">)</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="right" style="text-align: right; padding-right: 0"><mi>B</mi><mo>:</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace></mtd><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 0"><mo form="prefix" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="false">)</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="right" style="text-align: right; padding-right: 0"><mi>C</mi><mo>:</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace></mtd><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 0"><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p>
<p>The area of the triangle is</p>
<p><!-- $$ \frac{1}{2}\, (1)\, \left( \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \right) = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} $$ -->
<math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mo form="prefix" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="false">)</mo><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></math></p>
<p>This is the same as the area of the square, so</p>
<p><!-- $$ s = \frac{3^{1/4}}{2} $$ -->
<math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></math></p>
<p>This will be the length of <!-- $\bar{EF}$ --><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo accent="true">——</mo></mover></math>.</p>
<p>Points <em>D</em> and <em>E</em> are the midpoints of <em>AC</em> and <em>BC</em>:</p>
<p><!-- $$ \begin{align*}
D:\quad & \left( \frac{1}{4}, \, \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} \right) \\
E:\quad & \left( \frac{3}{4}, \, \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} \right)
\end{align*} $$ -->
<math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="right" style="text-align: right; padding-right: 0"><mi>D</mi><mo>:</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace></mtd><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 0"><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="right" style="text-align: right; padding-right: 0"><mi>E</mi><mo>:</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace></mtd><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 0"><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p>
<p>Since we know the length of Line <em>EF</em> and the position of Point <em>E</em>, we can calculate the position of Point <em>F</em> along Line <em>AB</em>:</p>
<p><!-- $$ F:\quad \left( \frac{1}{4} \, \left[ 3-\sqrt{4
   \sqrt{3}-3} \right], \, 0 \right) $$ -->
<math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>:</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>4</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></p>
<p>As I said, it’s kind of messy, but that’s what Mathematica is for. To six decimal places, the <em>x</em> coordinate is 0.254508. This is almost directly under Point <em>D</em>, but not quite.</p>
<p>Now we need the position of Point <em>G</em>. As it happens, it’s exactly <!-- $\frac{1}{2}$ --><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> unit to the right of Point <em>F</em>:</p>
<p><!-- $$ G:\quad \left( \frac{1}{4} \, \left[ 5 - \sqrt{4
   \sqrt{3}-3} \right], \, 0 \right) $$ -->
<math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>:</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo form="prefix" stretchy="true">[</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>4</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></p>
<p>To six decimal places, this is 0.754508.</p>
<p>How do we know this? Consider this drawing:</p>
<p><img alt="Second green triangle" class="ss" src="https://leancrew.com/all-this/images2026/20261026-Second%20green%20triangle.png" title="Second green triangle" width="80%"/></p>
<p>The light green triangle, <em>DEH</em>, is congruent to the dark green triangle, <em>GFJ</em>. We know this because:</p>
<ol>
<li>The right angle at <em>H</em> matches the right angle at <em>J</em>.</li>
<li>The length of <em>DH</em> is equal to the length of <em>GJ</em> (see the discussion above).</li>
<li>The angle at <em>D</em> matches the angle at <em>G</em> because <em>DE</em> is parallel to <em>FG</em> and <em>DH</em> is parallel to <em>GJ</em>.</li>
</ol>
<p>We therefore have an angle-side-angle congruence. The light green triangle is a duplicate of the dark green triangle, rotated 180° and moved up so its horizontal side is aligned with <em>DE</em>. The distance between <em>D</em> and <em>E</em> is <!-- $\frac{1}{2}$ --><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, so that must also be the distance between <em>F</em> and <em>G</em>.</p>
<p>With the locations of <em>F</em> and <em>G</em> known, we can find <em>H</em> and <em>J</em> by running lines perpendicular to <em>EF</em> that intersect <em>F</em> and <em>G</em>. The full equations for the positions of <em>H</em> and <em>J</em> are even messier than those of <em>F</em> and <em>G</em>, so I’ll just give the six-place decimal approximations:</p>
<p><!-- $$ \begin{align*}
H:\quad &(0.466506, 0.185267) \\
J:\quad &(0.538001, 0.247746)
\end{align*} $$ -->
<math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="right" style="text-align: right; padding-right: 0"><mi>H</mi><mo>:</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace></mtd><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 0"><mo form="prefix" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.466506</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.185267</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="false">)</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="right" style="text-align: right; padding-right: 0"><mi>J</mi><mo>:</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace></mtd><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left; padding-left: 0"><mo form="prefix" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.538001</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.247746</mn><mo form="postfix" stretchy="false">)</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p>
